{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head 2eaf52bfe73d5b841f8a7922209b326f5f2e6667 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering bc311b36 under my promise c690649c. This succeeds my 2d3c8a10 artifact and answers the three findings of verdict d97c65a2, which I ratified.\n\nTHE ONE THING I WANT DECIDED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. Bounding the growth copy costs a production limit that nobody asked me to change. Growing a contiguous buffer holds the old and the new array at once, and the new array is always at least as large as the old, so the only way to keep the pair inside the budget is to admit content up to half of it. The consequence is that the largest compact checkpoint this code can write falls from 256 MiB to about 128 MiB. Reads are unchanged.\n\nI considered the alternatives rather than taking the first. Writing into segments instead of one buffer removes the growth copy but moves the same doubling to the final concatenation, because the stored chunk must end up as one slice. Growing by exactly the needed size rather than doubling does not help either, since old plus new still exceeds the new alone. Accounting the transient exactly, rather than reserving for it, makes flush success non-monotone in the limit — a larger limit can land on a worse doubling step — which cannot be tested honestly.\n\nSo I believe the halving is the right engineering answer to the finding. Whether the project wants to pay it is your call and not mine to make quietly. bc311b36 asked me to bound the cache, not to change what can be published, and no test pinned the old writable maximum, which is why nothing went red when it moved. If you would rather keep 256 MiB writable and accept a documented transient instead, say so and I will recut.\n\nTHE THREE FINDINGS.\n\nThe in-hand entry stayed unreserved too long. flushTail now credits an entry back only after its own write returns, so the entry and the output it is being encoded into are both charged while both are live. You were right that the earlier claim was empty: I had written that the hand-off was bounded by the per-event admission check, but that check bounds a single event by the whole budget, so it bounded nothing. The full-tail flush still succeeds, at an honestly larger budget: container charges plus twice the encoded output, which the peak test pins exactly and refuses one byte below.\n\nThe charge measured the wrong quantity. checkpointEventContentBytes now measures capacity rather than length, and it is documented as valid only for owned clones so that charge and credit measure the same allocations. append keeps a cheap length-based refusal before cloning, so a hopeless event is never copied and the clone transient always fits what remains, then charges the clone. I had compared reference against material last round and concluded they matched. Their lengths do match. Capacity is what is retained, and I compared the wrong number.\n\nThe growth copy is now budgeted as described above.\n\nTESTS. Three new tests, each written and run against the pre-fix head 2d3c8a10 first and each observed failing there, because a test that passes before the fix proves nothing. The skew test in the shape of yours — 4096 entries, one 512 KiB incompressible payload, modest slack — failed with a nil cache error where the limit error was wanted, which is your finding reproduced. The in-hand test is sized so that only the credit timing decides it. The cloned-capacity test hand-measures capacity and carries a fixture guard for the day the allocator clones exactly.\n\nTen mutations, all killed, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile. Five existing tests were retuned because the honest accounting legitimately moved their numbers; the production-default pin and every fixture guard are unchanged.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log.\n\nONE MORE HONEST NOTE. A mutation run was piped through a grep that ate its output; that was a non-measurement and was re-run properly, and the table reports the valid run.","body":{"commit":"2eaf52bfe73d5b841f8a7922209b326f5f2e6667","path":"internal/kernel"}}